## DESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(Probability)
## DBchapter(Continuous distributions)
## DBsection(Application of a normal distribution)
## Institution(The College of Idaho)
## Author(R Cruz, L Danielson, J Guild)
## MLT(Temperature)
## Level(3)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('The Basic Practice of Statistics')
## AuthorText1('David Moore')
## EditionText1('5e')
## Section1('3')
## KEYWORDS('statistic', 'normal distribution', 'z score')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',  'PGML.pl',
    'answerHints.pl', 'PGstatisticsmacros.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

#  Setup

$meanF  = random(900, 1050, 25);
$sigmaF = random(40,  60,   10);

$temp1   = random(1090, 1150, 5);
$zscore4 = ($temp1 - $meanF) / $sigmaF;
$pval2   = .0001 * int(10000 * (uprob(abs($zscore4)) + .00005));

$ans = Compute($pval2)->with(tolerance => 0.005, tolType => 'absolute');
$cmp = $ans->cmp->withPostFilter(AnswerHints(
    sub {
        my ($correct, $student, $anshash) = @_;
        return abs($student - $correct) < .01;
    } => ["Close!"]
));

BEGIN_PGML
The temperature at any random location in a kiln used in the manufacture of bricks 
is normally distributed with a mean of [$meanF] and a standard deviation of [$sigmaF] degrees.

If bricks are fired at a temperature above [$temp1], they will crack and must 
be disposed of.  If the bricks are placed randomly throughout the kiln, the 
proportion of bricks that crack during the firing process is closest to
[_]{$cmp}

*Note: Please enter your answer in decimal form. For example, 5.02% should be entered as 0.0502.*
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
There are a number of ways to do this problem. We will find the [`z`]-score and then look up the value on a standard normal table or using software.

Recall that a [`z`]-score for a give data value [`x`] that comes from a normal distribution with mean [`\mu`] and standard deviation [`\sigma`] is 

[``` z = \frac{x-\mu}{\sigma}```]

Therefore for this problem,
[``` z = \frac{[$temp1]-[$meanF]}{[$sigmaF]} = [$zscore4] ```]
and then we seek the value:
[``` P(Z > [$zscore4]) = [$ans] ```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();       # This should be the last executable line in the problem.